This is memory-bound at n = 16M, since it streams 128 MB of input. So the main lever is breaking the serial floating-point add dependency chain that a straightforward reference would have. I couldn't compile or run it.

- **Vector path:** AVX2 with FMA, four independent 8-lane accumulators, 32 floats per iteration. Unaligned loads keep it correct for any pointers.
- **Accuracy:** The accumulators are reduced into a `double` every 4096 elements. This stops float rounding error from building up over 16M terms and keeps the result well inside the 1e-6 · Σ|a·b| + 1e-3 tolerance.
- **Tail and fallback:** Leftover elements, and the whole call when AVX2/FMA isn't available at compile time, run through a scalar loop that accumulates in `double`. Empty and tiny inputs go through that loop too.
- **Prefetching:** I left it to the hardware prefetchers, since this is a sequential stream.

<answer>
```c
#include <immintrin.h>

float dot_f32(const float *a, const float *b, size_t n)
{
    double total = 0.0;
    size_t i = 0;
#if defined(__AVX2__) && defined(__FMA__)
    while (i + 32 <= n) {
        size_t m = (n - i) / 32 * 32;
        if (m > 4096) m = 4096;
        __m256 s0 = _mm256_setzero_ps();
        __m256 s1 = _mm256_setzero_ps();
        __m256 s2 = _mm256_setzero_ps();
        __m256 s3 = _mm256_setzero_ps();
        const float *pa = a + i;
        const float *pb = b + i;
        for (size_t j = 0; j < m; j += 32) {
            s0 = _mm256_fmadd_ps(_mm256_loadu_ps(pa + j),      _mm256_loadu_ps(pb + j),      s0);
            s1 = _mm256_fmadd_ps(_mm256_loadu_ps(pa + j + 8),  _mm256_loadu_ps(pb + j + 8),  s1);
            s2 = _mm256_fmadd_ps(_mm256_loadu_ps(pa + j + 16), _mm256_loadu_ps(pb + j + 16), s2);
            s3 = _mm256_fmadd_ps(_mm256_loadu_ps(pa + j + 24), _mm256_loadu_ps(pb + j + 24), s3);
        }
        __m256 s = _mm256_add_ps(_mm256_add_ps(s0, s1), _mm256_add_ps(s2, s3));
        float t[8];
        _mm256_storeu_ps(t, s);
        total += (double)t[0] + t[1] + t[2] + t[3] + t[4] + t[5] + t[6] + t[7];
        i += m;
    }
#endif
    for (; i < n; i++)
        total += (double)a[i] * (double)b[i];
    return (float)total;
}
```
