The function checks the data in 64-byte blocks. A block is uniform if all eight 64-bit words equal the first byte repeated. A uniform block adds 64 to one counter, so long runs cost almost nothing. Any other block falls back to a 4-table histogram, which avoids store-forwarding stalls from repeated bytes hitting the same counter. The tail bytes use the same tables, and the four tables are merged into `counts` at the end. I couldn't compile or run this, so it is untested.

The tables are 64-bit and live on the stack (8 KB), well under the 4 MiB limit. Empty and tiny inputs skip the block loop and are handled by the tail loop.

