This version uses AVX2 and non-temporal stores. I haven't compiled or run it. At n = 16M the job is memory-bound, so the main gain should come from streaming stores, which skip reading the output lines before writing them.

- **Head:** a scalar loop runs until `out` is 32-byte aligned, so the streaming stores are legal.
- **Within a vector:** two shift-and-add steps compute the prefix sum of 4 lanes. These use `_mm256_permute4x64_epi64` and a blend with zero.
- **Carry chain:** the running carry is a broadcast vector. Each iteration adds the vector's own total (computed independently of the carry), so the loop-carried dependency is a single add per 4 elements.
- **Tail:** a scalar loop finishes the last few elements. An `_mm_sfence()` follows the streaming stores. Without AVX2, the code falls back to the plain scalar loop.

<answer>
```c
#include <immintrin.h>

void prefix_sum_i64(const int64_t *in, int64_t *out, size_t n)
{
    size_t i = 0;
    int64_t s = 0;
#ifdef __AVX2__
    if (n >= 16) {
        while (i < n && (((uintptr_t)(out + i)) & 31)) {
            s += in[i];
            out[i] = s;
            i++;
        }
        const __m256i zero = _mm256_setzero_si256();
        __m256i carry = _mm256_set1_epi64x(s);
        for (; i + 4 <= n; i += 4) {
            __m256i x = _mm256_loadu_si256((const __m256i *)(in + i));
            __m256i t = _mm256_blend_epi32(zero, _mm256_permute4x64_epi64(x, 0x90), 0xFC);
            x = _mm256_add_epi64(x, t);
            t = _mm256_blend_epi32(zero, _mm256_permute4x64_epi64(x, 0x40), 0xF0);
            x = _mm256_add_epi64(x, t);
            __m256i bc = _mm256_permute4x64_epi64(x, 0xFF);
            _mm256_stream_si256((__m256i *)(out + i), _mm256_add_epi64(x, carry));
            carry = _mm256_add_epi64(carry, bc);
        }
        s = _mm256_extract_epi64(carry, 0);
        for (; i < n; i++) {
            s += in[i];
            out[i] = s;
        }
        _mm_sfence();
        return;
    }
#endif
    for (; i < n; i++) {
        s += in[i];
        out[i] = s;
    }
}
```
