The histogram works on 64-byte blocks. If every byte in a block equals its first byte, the block extends a run and only a register run-counter changes (an AVX2 compare against a broadcast of the first byte). The run is flushed to the table when the byte value changes. Any other block goes through four scalar sub-histograms, so back-to-back increments to the same counter don't serialize on store-forwarding. The tail is handled bytewise. Without AVX2 the code falls back to the scalar path. I haven't compiled or run it.

